# tools/cr/crypto_count_guard_witness.rish — the count-guard loom for the Season G crypto library. The lantern the reds-first law names:
# a crypto file-count claim drifted from memory twice (REDS %77, then #80 across three living docs at once), so a lantern that fires
# twice becomes a loom. This witness computes the count rather than trusting a typed word, and asserts the measures agree: every
# per-file witness the suite registers has its crypto/<name>.rye, every crypto/<name>.rye is registered in the suite, and the two
# counts match. A file and its proof can never disagree again without this going RED.
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md.
# Kin: tools/cr/crypto_suite_witness.rish (the choir this guards), crypto/README.md and docs/CRYPTO.md (the front doors whose spelled
# counts this witness gives an authoritative number to update from). Booked at work-in-progress/REDS.md #80.
#
# This authors no cryptography — it counts and compares. Purely local: no key, no signature, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_count_guard_witness.rish

say "crypto-count-guard: Language EN — the count-guard loom: the number of crypto/*.rye files and the number the suite registers must be one number, computed here rather than typed into prose. Booked by REDS %80 after a file-count claim drifted from memory twice."
say "crypto-count-guard: Style Radiant — one measure, two ways: files on disk and registrations in the choir. When they agree, the library's own front-door count is whatever this witness prints — a source of truth for the spelled numbers in crypto/README.md and docs/CRYPTO.md, so no doc need ever guess again."
say "crypto-count-guard: Lens TAME — every crypto/<name>.rye is asserted registered in the suite, and every per-file witness the suite registers (excluding the suite itself and this guard) is asserted to carry its .rye — a bijection, not a bare tally — then the two counts are asserted equal. RED names exactly which name broke the bijection. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let mk = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin"]
assert mk.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not make crypto/bin — ${mk.err}"

# The crypto/<name>.rye source names, one per line, sorted. Real files only (-type f): a cross-directory composition like
# crypto/bitcoin_address.rye imports encoding/ modules through flat-named symlink shims that live in crypto/ (rye resolves nested
# imports relative to the importing path), and those shims are not primitives the suite proves — counting them would break the bijection.
let list_all = run ["sh" "-c" "find crypto -maxdepth 1 -name '*.rye' -type f | sed 's:.*/::; s:.rye$::' | sort > crypto/bin/.cc_all.txt"]
assert list_all.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not list crypto/*.rye — ${list_all.err}"

# THE SECOND CLASS, and the fault that taught it (REDS %191). The bijection above assumes every regular crypto/*.rye is a primitive the
# suite can build and read a `GREEN crypto-<name>` line from. crypto/sha3_digest.rye is a command-line program that prints a hex digest,
# so the suite has no GREEN line to read from it, and this guard read RED while standing off the standing roster -- unrun, so nobody heard
# it, while both front doors went on reciting a count it would have corrected. It cannot simply move: rye resolves a nested import relative
# to the importing file's own directory, so a module importing sha3.rye by bare name stands beside it by the language's rule.
#
# So the tool class is DECLARED, in tools/fixtures/crypto_tool_modules.txt, and a row is a REDIRECT rather than an excuse. Three assertions
# below make it one: the declared file exists, the witness that proves it exists, and that witness stands on the standing roster. A redirect
# pointing nowhere reds exactly like an unproven file, which is what keeps this from being a bypass with a comment over it.
let tools_decl = "tools/fixtures/crypto_tool_modules.txt"
let have_decl = run ["sh" "-c" "test -f ${tools_decl} && echo yes || echo no"]
assert (trim have_decl.out) == "yes" else "crypto-count-guard: the tool declaration is missing at ${tools_decl}; without it this guard cannot tell a tool from an unproven primitive"

let list_tools = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*(#|$)' ${tools_decl} | awk '{print $1}' | sort -u > crypto/bin/.cc_tools.txt"]
assert list_tools.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not read the tool declaration — ${list_tools.err}"

# The three assertions, run by tools/fixtures/crypto_tool_declaration_scan.sh so the loop lives where a shell variable is a shell
# variable. Each is gated at zero: a declared module with no file on disk, a redirect to a witness that is not a file, and -- the one
# this round exists for -- a redirect to a guard the standing roster does not name. An unrun guard guards nothing, so a tool excused
# from the suite must name a proof a lap actually runs, or the declaration would launder an unproven file into a green count.
let decl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/crypto_tool_declaration_scan.sh"]
assert decl.ok else "crypto-count-guard: the tool-declaration scan must complete -- see stderr"
say decl.out
assert decl.out contains "malformed=0" else "crypto-count-guard: a tool declaration row does not carry exactly three fields (module, witness, roster guard)"
assert decl.out contains "ghost_module=0" else "crypto-count-guard: the tool declaration names a crypto/<name>.rye that does not stand on disk"
assert decl.out contains "missing_witness=0" else "crypto-count-guard: a declared tool redirects to a proving witness that is not a file"
assert decl.out contains "unrostered=0" else "crypto-count-guard: a declared tool is proven by a guard the standing roster does not name, so nothing runs it"
assert decl.out contains "verdict=ok" else "crypto-count-guard: the tool declaration must be sound before its rows are believed"

# And the declaration's own refusals proven on metal, since a reading that only ever passes cannot be told from one that reads nothing.
# Fourteen behaviors on real trees in a throwaway pen: nine refusals bitten -- a module nobody wrote, a redirect to no file, a guard the
# roster does not name, a two-field row, a four-field row, a guard name one letter short, a guard name that is only a prefix of a real
# one, three faults at once, and an absent declaration -- and five honest readings left free.
let decl_control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/crypto_tool_declaration_control.sh"]
assert decl_control.ok else "crypto-count-guard: the tool-declaration control must complete -- see stderr"
assert decl_control.out contains "cases_fail=0" else "crypto-count-guard: a planted declaration case did not behave -- ${decl_control.out}"
assert decl_control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "crypto-count-guard: the declaration control must agree with itself before its exemptions are believed"

# The primitives are what remains once the declared tools step aside, and that is the set the bijection reads.
let list_files = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -Fxv -f crypto/bin/.cc_tools.txt crypto/bin/.cc_all.txt > crypto/bin/.cc_files.txt || true"]
assert list_files.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not separate primitives from tools — ${list_files.err}"

# The per-file witnesses the suite registers, one name per line, sorted, excluding the suite and this guard.
let list_reg = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -oE 'crypto_[a-z0-9_]+_witness' tools/cr/crypto_suite_witness.rish | grep -vE 'suite|count_guard' | sed 's:^crypto_::; s:_witness$::' | sort -u > crypto/bin/.cc_reg.txt"]
assert list_reg.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not read the suite registrations — ${list_reg.err}"

# Bijection A: every crypto/<name>.rye is registered in the suite (grep -Fxv returns the unmatched names; || true keeps ok on empty).
let unreg = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -Fxv -f crypto/bin/.cc_reg.txt crypto/bin/.cc_files.txt || true"]
assert unreg.ok else "crypto-count-guard: bijection scan A failed — ${unreg.err}"
assert (trim unreg.out) == "" else "crypto-count-guard: crypto/<name>.rye not registered in the suite — ${unreg.out}"

# Bijection B: every suite registration has its crypto/<name>.rye on disk.
let orphan = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -Fxv -f crypto/bin/.cc_files.txt crypto/bin/.cc_reg.txt || true"]
assert orphan.ok else "crypto-count-guard: bijection scan B failed — ${orphan.err}"
assert (trim orphan.out) == "" else "crypto-count-guard: suite registers a witness with no crypto/<name>.rye — ${orphan.out}"

# The two counts must be one number.
let files_c = run ["sh" "-c" "wc -l < crypto/bin/.cc_files.txt | tr -d ' \n'"]
assert files_c.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not count files — ${files_c.err}"
let n_files = trim files_c.out

let reg_c = run ["sh" "-c" "wc -l < crypto/bin/.cc_reg.txt | tr -d ' \n'"]
assert reg_c.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not count registrations — ${reg_c.err}"
let n_reg = trim reg_c.out

# The three numbers the front doors quote, computed here so no page has to guess again. `n_all` counts regular files only, which is why it
# reads 82 while `ls crypto/*.rye` reads 87: five of the eighty-seven are seam symlinks into encoding/, and a symlink is a module a READER
# finds in this directory rather than a primitive this suite proves. crypto/MODULES.md carries all 87 and is held to the directory by
# tools/cr/crypto_module_roster_witness.rish; this guard holds the 82 the suite is responsible for.
let all_c = run ["sh" "-c" "wc -l < crypto/bin/.cc_all.txt | tr -d ' \n'"]
assert all_c.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not count regular files — ${all_c.err}"
let n_all = trim all_c.out

let tool_c = run ["sh" "-c" "wc -l < crypto/bin/.cc_tools.txt | tr -d ' \n'"]
assert tool_c.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not count declared tools — ${tool_c.err}"
let n_tools = trim tool_c.out

assert n_files == n_reg else "crypto-count-guard: crypto/*.rye files (${n_files}) != per-file witnesses the suite registers (${n_reg})"

# THE THIRD FIRING BECAME A WIDER LOOM (REDS %105). The bijection above catches a file that is not registered; it did NOT catch the suite
# RECITING a stale count in its own prose. crypto_suite_witness.rish printed "all seventy-four Season G crypto files ... the count guard
# confirms the file count" while this guard measured 81 -- a claim citing its own auditor as agreeing with it, when the auditor said
# otherwise. That is REDS %77 and %80's family a third time, so the loom widens: no count may be recited in the suite at all. The number
# lives here, computed, and every doc and claim line points at it rather than repeating it.
let recite = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -nEi '(one|two|three|four|five|six|seven|eight|nine|ten|twenty|thirty|forty|fifty|sixty|seventy|eighty|ninety|hundred|[0-9]+)[a-z-]* (season g )?(crypto )?files' tools/cr/crypto_suite_witness.rish || true"]
assert recite.ok else "crypto-count-guard: could not scan the suite for a recited count -- ${recite.err}"
assert (trim recite.out) == "" else "crypto-count-guard: the suite RECITES a file count in its own prose, which is exactly what drifted three times -- remove the number and point at this guard instead. Offending lines: ${recite.out}"

say "GREEN crypto-count-guard — ${n_all} regular crypto/*.rye files: ${n_files} primitives, each registered in the suite and registering a real file, and ${n_tools} declared tool(s) whose proving witness exists and stands on the standing roster. The suite recites no count of its own so none can drift (REDS %105), and a declared tool is a redirect rather than an excuse (REDS %191)."
